 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed edits are pushed over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Five-second polling offers simpler stateless operation but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service reduces connection-management operations but introduces recurring vendor fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets provide fast updates without vendor lock-in, in exchange for owning connection capacity, reliable publication, and browser reconnect/catch-up behavior. See [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
